# Valgrind suppressions for the dftracer-utils C++ tests.
#
# Keep this list small and justified. To add an entry, reproduce the report
# with `make valgrind-cpp` (the runner passes --gen-suppressions=all, so the
# logs in build/valgrind-logs/cpp/*.log already contain paste-ready stanzas)
# and only suppress noise that is provably outside our control (libc/loader,
# zlib internals, etc.) -- never a real leak in our own code.

# --- Dynamic loader / libc one-time allocations -----------------------------
{
   dl-init-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   fun:_dl_init
}
{
   dl-open-reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   fun:_dl_catch_exception
   fun:_dl_open
}

# --- zlib: inflate/deflate may read in word-sized chunks --------------------
# zlib intentionally over-reads within allocated buffers in some builds; these
# are well-known false positives, not bugs in our reader code.
{
   zlib-inflate-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   obj:*/libz.so*
}
{
   zlib-inflate-value8
   Memcheck:Value8
   ...
   obj:*/libz.so*
}

# --- Vectorscan: SIMD scans read whole vectors past the end of the input ---
# Vectorscan reads the haystack in aligned vector loads and masks the bytes
# past its end, so Memcheck sees branches on bytes never written. The scan
# entry point is hs_scan; the frames above it are its internal engines.
{
   vectorscan-scan-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:hs_scan
}
{
   vectorscan-scan-value8
   Memcheck:Value8
   ...
   fun:hs_scan
}

# --- simdjson: stage 2 reads string padding in whole vectors -----------------
# A dstd::String is parsed in place when its spare capacity covers the simdjson
# padding, as simdjson does for a std::string. That capacity is never written,
# and the string scan loads it in vector reads and masks it, so Memcheck sees a
# branch on bytes past the input.
{
   simdjson-stage2-padding-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:*dom_parser_implementation*stage2*
}

# --- derive_io_size: GCC reads the payload of an empty optional ---------------
# GCC turns each `opt && *opt > 0` check into a branch-free AND that loads the
# payload even when the optional is empty. The result cannot depend on that
# payload, but Memcheck sees a branch on bytes never written.
{
   derive-io-size-empty-optional-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:derive_io_size
}
